<ansi fg="black-bold">.:</ansi> <ansi fg="magenta">Help for </ansi><ansi fg="command">burden</ansi>

In a fight, company members bear only their worn equipment. The heavier
your own load against what your Strength lets you move in, the more
<ansi fg="yellow">burdened</ansi> you are, and a burdened fighter dodges less.

<ansi fg="yellow-bold">Your own load, not the company's</ansi>

Your burden counts only your worn gear. All unworn items are shared cargo. The
company's cargo, your pack's extra room, and the horses carry for the
company on the road (<ansi fg="command">help cargo</ansi>), but never make you lighter on your
feet. Removing worn gear to cargo takes it off your back.

Your company members carry no personal backpack. Foes still bear their own worn and carried gear.

<ansi fg="yellow-bold">How much you can move in</ansi>

  {{ config "GamePlay.Combat.AgilityBaseKg" }} kg, plus {{ config "GamePlay.Combat.AgilityStrengthKg" }} kg for each point of Strength.

The first {{ config "GamePlay.Combat.AgilityFreeLoad" }} of that (a share of the whole: 0.35 means 35%) costs
nothing. Past it your burden grows until you reach the full amount, and
stays at its worst beyond it. Because it is a share of what you can move
in, a strong fighter wears the same armor more lightly than a weak one.

With the usual settings and Strength 10, you move in 20 kg: the first
7 kg is free, and at 20 kg or more you are heavily burdened.

<ansi fg="yellow-bold">What it does</ansi>

  <ansi fg="yellow">unburdened</ansi>          your full dodge
  <ansi fg="yellow">lightly burdened</ansi>    a little less
  <ansi fg="yellow">burdened</ansi>            noticeably less
  <ansi fg="yellow">heavily burdened</ansi>    as little as two fifths of your dodge

Only the dodge suffers. A shield's block and a weapon's parry are not
touched, so an armored fighter with a shield or a blade still defends
well. When you could parry or dodge a blow, the better chance is rolled,
so a burdened swordsman parries more often than they dodge
(<ansi fg="command">help defense</ansi>).

<ansi fg="yellow-bold">Where you see it</ansi>

- <ansi fg="command">status</ansi> shows your burden in the Vitals panel. The Company panel's
  Load is the company's travel load, a different thing (<ansi fg="command">help cargo</ansi>).
- <ansi fg="command">look [someone]</ansi> shows theirs: a companion, another player, or a foe.
- <ansi fg="command">scout [group]</ansi> and <ansi fg="command">look [group]</ansi> name the foes who are burdened.
- In the web client, the Character tab's Overview shows yours.

Nothing about burden is saved: it changes the moment you put something
on, take it off, pick it up, or stow it.

See also: <ansi fg="command">help defense</ansi>, <ansi fg="command">help perception</ansi>, <ansi fg="command">help strength</ansi>, <ansi fg="command">help cargo</ansi>,
<ansi fg="command">help combat</ansi>.
